course: dictionary
lesson: 9377
title: Sportsmanship: playing fair and kind
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sportsmanship is a noun, so it names a quality. It is how you behave in sport and games. Read the context: «Sportsmanship means playing fairly, winning without boasting and losing without sulking.» The sentence gives you three clues: fair play, a modest winner, and a gracious loser.
explain: Teachers use the word when they praise good behaviour. A player who shakes hands after a match, helps someone who has fallen, or does not cheat is showing sportsmanship. Look for the action words around the word to find its meaning.
explain: Sportsmanship is uncountable, so you say «good sportsmanship» or «show sportsmanship», not «a sportsmanship». Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If «playing fairly and kindly» still makes sense in place of the word, you have it.
fact: Sportsmanship is a noun meaning playing fairly and kindly in sport or games.
fact: «Sportsmanship» means following the rules and respecting the other team and the referee.
fact: Being a good winner means not boasting, and being a good loser means not sulking.
fact: Phrases to remember: «good sportsmanship», «show sportsmanship», «sportsmanship award».
fact: Sportsmanship is an uncountable noun, so it has no plural and does not take «a» in front of it.
exercise: «Sportsmanship means playing fairly, winning without boasting and losing without sulking.» What does sportsmanship mean? | Running faster than everyone else | *Playing fairly and kindly, as a good winner and a good loser | Winning at any cost, even by cheating | Being the referee in a match
exercise: Find and copy one word from «winning without boasting and losing without sulking» that means showing off about a win. | losing | sulking | winning | *boasting
exercise: Sam's team lost, but he shook hands with the other team and said, «Well played.» What does this show? | Cheating | Boasting | Sulking | *Good sportsmanship
test: In «losing without sulking», what does sulking mean? | *Being cross and moody, and not speaking nicely | Laughing loudly at a joke | Helping a player who has fallen | Shaking hands with the winners
test: Which action shows sportsmanship? | Sulking in the changing room | *Helping a player who fell over | Cheating when the referee looks away | Boasting about your goals
exam: Which word means playing fairly, winning kindly and losing well in sport? | *Sportsmanship | Sulking | Boasting | Cheating
